{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_ajetgfaq4c7sbqymac27eshyyoj4vzbdqhugsvoju7li6lohxoyq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01Xm8iXKWkydHAUvV6HueoLP","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-D30ByW/translation/ERRORS.md","content":"# ERRORS.md — Phase C error-surface table\n\nDerived mechanically from `c_src/src/lib.c`. Grep results:\n\n```sh\ngrep -n 'assert\\|RETURN_ERROR\\|return -1\\|return NULL\\|errno' src/lib.c   # -> 0 hits\ngrep -n 'if (!\\|<= 0\\|> 0\\|== 0'                              src/lib.c   # -> 14 hits (listed below)\n```\n\n**There is no error enum, no `assert`, and no error return code anywhere in\nthe library.** Every public function either returns a value unconditionally\nor returns `void`. The \"rejection surface\" therefore consists of:\n\n* **defensive null checks** that silently substitute a fallback instead of\n  failing (`c2GJK`'s six pointer checks),\n* **`switch` / `default:` fall-throughs** that swallow out-of-domain\n  `count` and out-of-domain `C2_TYPE` enum values,\n* **unguarded divisions and square roots** whose sentinel is `inf` / `NaN`\n  rather than an error code,\n* **unvalidated indices / counts** read straight out of caller memory.\n\nEach row below is one distinct such branch. \"Expected C result\" is the exact\nobservable behaviour of the C, which the Rust must reproduce bit-for-bit.\n\n| #  | function | trigger (exact invalid input / condition) | expected C result |\n|----|----------|--------------------------------------------|-------------------|\n| 1  | `c2GJK` | `ax_ptr == NULL` | no deref; `ax = c2xIdentity()` = `{p:(0,0), r:{c:1,s:0}}`; runs normally |\n| 2  | `c2GJK` | `bx_ptr == NULL` | no deref; `bx = c2xIdentity()`; runs normally |\n| 3  | `c2GJK` | `outA == NULL` | `*outA` not written; return value + other outs unaffected |\n| 4  | `c2GJK` | `outB == NULL` | `*outB` not written; return value + other outs unaffected |\n| 5  | `c2GJK` | `iterations == NULL` | `*iterations` not written; return value unaffected |\n| 6  | `c2GJK` | `cache == NULL` | cache neither read nor written; simplex seeded fresh (`count=1`, `div=1`) |\n| 7  | `c2GJK` | `cache != NULL` but `cache->count == 0` | `cache_was_good == 0` → skips the read block → fresh seed; cache *is* written on exit |\n| 8  | `c2GJK` | `cache->count != 0` and `!(min<max*2 && metric < -1e8)` — i.e. essentially always | quirk: `cache_was_read = 1`, the stale simplex is trusted verbatim (no re-seed) |\n| 9  | `c2GJK` | `cache->count != 0` and `min_metric < max_metric*2 && metric < -1.0e8f` (needs a metric ≤ −1e8) | `cache_was_read` stays 0 → simplex re-seeded from vertex 0 |\n| 10 | `c2GJK` | warm cache with `cache->count == 3` (valid indices) | cache read, `s.count==3` on entry → first loop iteration hits `if (s.count==3)` → `hit=1`, `iter==0`, `dist==0`, `a==b` |\n| 11 | `c2GJK` | `cache->count < 0` (e.g. `-1`, `-1000`) | copy loop body never runs; `s.count` = the negative value; `c2GJKSimplexMetric` `default:`→0; `switch(s.count)` matches nothing; `count != 3`; `c2L` `default:`→(0,0); `c2D` `default:`→(0,0) → `dot(d,d)=0 < eps²` → break; `c2Witness` `default:` → `a=b=(0,0)`; `dist=0`; cache written back with the negative count (write loop skipped) |\n| 12 | `c2GJK` | `cache->count == 4` | reads `cache->iA[3]` / `cache->iB[3]`, which by struct layout alias `cache->iB[0]` and `cache->div`; writes `verts[3]` (= `s.d`, still in bounds). Fully deterministic, must match |\n| 13 | `c2GJK` | `cache->count > 4` (e.g. 5, 6) | out-of-bounds read past `iB[3]` and out-of-bounds write past `s.d` into `s.div`/`s.count`. Deterministic for a given layout; compared as-is |\n| 14 | `c2GJK` | `cache->iA[i]` / `cache->iB[i]` ≥ proxy `count` but < 8 (e.g. index 3 on a `C2_TYPE_CIRCLE` proxy whose `count==1`) | index is inside `c2Proxy::verts[8]` so no OOB, but the slot was never initialised by `c2MakeProxy` → **indeterminate in C** (reads uninitialised stack). Documented, not asserted |\n| 15 | `c2GJK` | `cache->iA[i]` ≥ 8 (e.g. 100) | genuine out-of-bounds read past `c2Proxy::verts` — UB, indeterminate. Documented, not asserted |\n| 16 | `c2GJK` | `typeA` or `typeB` is an out-of-range enum value (`3`, `7`, `255`, `(C2_TYPE)-1`) | `c2MakeProxy`'s `switch` has **no `default:`** → the `c2Proxy` local is left entirely uninitialised → `radius`/`count`/`verts` are indeterminate stack garbage. **UB in C**; documented, not asserted. (The well-defined half — that `c2MakeProxy` writes *nothing* — is asserted in row 17.) |\n| 17 | `c2MakeProxy` | `type` not in `{0,1,2}` (`3`, `4`, `255`, `0xFFFFFFFF`) | `switch` falls through with no `default:` → **not a single byte of `*p` is written**; a caller-prefilled `c2Proxy` buffer is returned bit-identical |\n| 18 | `c2MakeProxy` | `shape == NULL` with a valid `type` | dereferences NULL → `SIGSEGV`. Both libs crash identically; not exercised in-process |\n| 19 | `c2BBVerts` | `out == NULL` or `bb == NULL` | NULL deref → `SIGSEGV`; not exercised in-process |\n| 20 | `c2Support` | `count <= 0` (`0`, `-1`, `INT_MIN`) | `verts[0]` is read *before* the loop guard, then `for (i=1; i<count)` never runs → returns `0` |\n| 21 | `c2Support` | `count == 1` | loop never runs → returns `0` regardless of `d` |\n| 22 | `c2Support` | all dots equal, or `d == (0,0)` (every dot `0`, never `> dmax`) | returns `0` (first index wins ties) |\n| 23 | `c2Support` | some `verts[i]` component is `NaN` | `dot > dmax` is false for NaN → NaN candidates never win; `dmax` can be NaN from `verts[0]`, after which *no* later index wins → returns `0` |\n| 24 | `c2Support` | `verts == NULL` | NULL deref → `SIGSEGV`; not exercised in-process |\n| 25 | `c2Witness` | `s->count` not in `{1,2,3}` (`0`, `4`, `-1`) | `default:` → `*a = (0,0)`, `*b = (0,0)` |\n| 26 | `c2Witness` | `s->div == 0` (with `count` 1..3) | `den = 1.0f/0 = +inf`; for `count==1` the outputs are unaffected; for `count` 2/3 outputs are `±inf` or `NaN` (`inf * 0.0f`) |\n| 27 | `c2Witness` | `s->div` is `NaN` or `-0.0` | `den` is `NaN` / `-inf`; propagates into the weighted sums |\n| 28 | `c2L` | `s->count` not in `{1,2}` (`3`, `0`, `4`, `-1`) | `default:` → returns `(0,0)` |\n| 29 | `c2L` | `s->div == 0`, `count` 2 | `den = +inf` → components `±inf` / `NaN` |\n| 30 | `c2D` | `s->count` == `3` or any value outside `{1,2}` | `default:` → returns `(0,0)` |\n| 31 | `c2D` | `count == 2` and `a.p == b.p` (degenerate edge) | `ab = (0,0)`, `det2 = 0` which is *not* `> 0` → takes `c2CCW90((0,0))` → `(0,0)` |\n| 32 | `c2GJKSimplexMetric` | `s->count` not in `{2,3}` (`1`, `0`, `4`, `-1`) | `default:` shares the `case 1:` body → returns `0` |\n| 33 | `c2GJKSimplexMetric` | `count == 2` with equal points | `c2Len((0,0)) = sqrtf(0) = 0` |\n| 34 | `c2Norm` | `a == (0,0)` | `c2Len = 0`; `c2Div(a, 0)` → `1.0f/0 = +inf`; `0 * inf` → `NaN` in both components |\n| 35 | `c2Norm` | `a` has a `NaN` or `inf` component | `c2Len` → `NaN`/`inf`; result `NaN` components |\n| 36 | `c2Div` | `b == 0` | `1.0f/0 = +inf` → components `±inf`, or `NaN` when the component is `0` |\n| 37 | `c2Div` | `b == -0.0` | `1.0f/-0.0 = -inf` → sign-flipped `inf` / `NaN` |\n| 38 | `c2Div` | `b == NaN` | both components `NaN` |\n| 39 | `c2Len` | `a` huge (e.g. `1e38`) | `c2Dot` overflows to `+inf`; `sqrtf(+inf) = +inf` |\n| 40 | `c2Len` | `a` has a `NaN` component | `sqrtf(NaN) = NaN` (no domain error, sign of NaN preserved by `sqrtss`) |\n| 41 | `c2Maxv` | either operand has a `NaN` component | `(a.x) > (b.x)` is false whenever a `NaN` is involved → **`b`'s component is returned**, not `a`'s. Non-commutative NaN ordering must be reproduced |\n| 42 | `c2Minv` | either operand has a `NaN` component | `(a.x) < (b.x)` is false → **`b`'s component is returned** |\n| 43 | `c2Maxv`/`c2Minv` | `+0.0` vs `-0.0` | neither `>` nor `<` holds → `b`'s component is returned, so the *sign of zero* depends on argument order |\n| 44 | `c2Clampv` |